## Three headings, two jobs

A map-reading lesson has two sections: “Find the contour lines” and “Estimate the slope.” Both start with an `h2`. Between them, the CMS inserts a quiz with an `h2` of its own: “Which path is steeper?” You want a generous pause before each lesson section. Inside the quiz, that same space would look like a missing question.

For a small example, give the article the class `.lesson`. Put each lesson heading in a section, and place an aside with the class `.quiz` between the two sections. Put the question heading and its possible answers inside the aside. That gives you three headings in reading order, all at the right level. There’s no reason to change the question’s heading level just to make its spacing different.

Here’s a reasonable first attempt at the lesson style:

```css
.lesson h2 {
  margin-block-start: 3rem;
}
```

Read that space between `.lesson` and `h2` as “somewhere inside.” The question is somewhere inside the lesson, too. Inspect its heading and you should find the same margin declaration that applies to “Find the contour lines.” The problem isn’t that the quiz has lost a contest over specificity. It’s that the lesson rule entered the contest at all.

## Draw the boundary around the quiz

Replace the first rule rather than adding to it. If you leave `.lesson h2` in the stylesheet, it will keep matching the question regardless of what the new rule does.

```css
@scope (.lesson) to (.quiz) {
  h2 {
    margin-block-start: 3rem;
  }
}
```

`.lesson` identifies the scope root. Within that root, `.quiz` identifies a lower limit: the quiz element itself and its descendants are outside this scope. The `h2` selector can still reach into the lesson’s sections. It doesn’t need the headings to be direct children, so an editor adding a wrapper around a section won’t break the rule.

Follow the three headings again. “Find the contour lines” is inside the root and outside the quiz, so it gets the margin. “Which path is steeper?” is under the limit, so the scoped rule has nothing to say about its margin. “Estimate the slope” gets the extra space even though it comes *after* the quiz. The limit excludes a branch of the document tree, not the rest of the document from that point on.

That distinction gives you a useful debugging check. If the last heading doesn’t pick up the rule, inspect its ancestors in the rendered page. A CMS template that accidentally puts the second section inside the aside has made it part of the excluded branch. Increasing the selector’s specificity won’t bring it back into scope.

## Change the font and follow a different path

Now give the lesson a bookish typeface. Leave the scoped spacing rule in place and add this ordinary rule alongside it:

```css
.lesson {
  font-family: Georgia, serif;
}
```

Assuming there are no competing font declarations, all three headings now use the lesson’s font family. That includes the question. Inspect the quiz heading: you’re looking for a font family inherited from an ancestor, not a `.lesson` selector matching the heading itself.

The font declaration applies to the article. `font-family` is inherited, so the aside takes its parent’s computed font family, and the question heading in turn takes the aside’s. Nothing about the scope limit changes who the heading’s parent is. By contrast, the extra margin is assigned directly to each matching heading. The scoped selector never matches the question, and margins aren’t inherited. The question keeps whatever spacing its own styles or the browser give it; exclusion doesn’t set its margin to zero.

If the quiz should read like a separate activity, set its typeface where that activity begins:

```css
.quiz {
  font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
}
```

Now the heading and ordinary question text can inherit from the quiz instead. You haven’t reset every descendant, so a code sample with its own monospace font can keep it. If the quiz is meant to share the lesson’s typography, skip this rule: the spacing boundary already does its job.

## Keep the extra space optional

Treat the larger margin as an enhancement. Start with readable heading spacing in your base stylesheet or retain the browser’s heading defaults. A browser that doesn’t understand `@scope` can ignore the block: the lesson will lose its extra section spacing, but the question and the section after it will still read in order. Don’t use the original `.lesson h2` rule as the fallback; that would bring back the very match you removed.

The supplied MDN reference describes `@scope` as Baseline Newly available. It’s stable CSS, not a guarantee about every browser your readers use; current support for your target browsers still needs verification. When reviewing the layout, compare all three headings rather than just checking the quiz. The useful outcome is a compact question *and* a clear return to the lesson.

